\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  End Mostly Down; Rush To Buy Dollars \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 09:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped steeply during local trading hours Monday as dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Japan triggered an end-of-the-month rush to acquire the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah and the Thai baht both finished Asian dealing down compared with levels seen Friday, as dollar-buying by local corporations in Bangkok and Jakarta dominated trade. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buying also weighed on the Philippine peso, while the sharpness of the Singapore dollar's decline led dealers to believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won, however, held its own, strengthening against the U.S. dollar as the country's strong economic performance continued to attract heavy inflows of foreign investment funds into the Seoul stock market. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also edged higher. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's action Monday to support the dollar and arrest the yen's appreciation sparked an early round of dollar-buying against the Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Heavy bids were seen from customers in Bangkok and Jakarta as regional corporations hastened to acquire dollars lest the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate further before their debt service obligations fall due at the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  Fearing a liquidity crunch ahead of the New Year, customers also bought dollars to meet December's debt payments, exaggerating the decline in the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Local corporations are buying in a last ditch attempt to get dollars before the month's end," said a dealer at one U.S. bank in Singapore. "And they are buying for December too. Demand is extra big this month." \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Asian trading hours the dollar had been pushed higher to trade against the baht at THB39.0350, up steeply from THB38.7650 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the rupiah the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had risen to IDR7,320, up from IDR7,200 Friday as fears of trouble in the restive provinces of Aceh and Irian Jaya further fueled demand. \par \par \uc2  Against the Philippine peso, the dollar rose to close at PHP40.935, up from PHP40.835 at the end of Friday's session, despite a rise in government debt yields at the weekly auction. \par \par \uc2  The weight of bids to buy the U.S, dollar against the Singapore dollar forced some market participants to conclude that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, acting through local and locally-active foreign banks to push the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \par \par \uc2  Dealers suggested that the MAS was keen to emphasize its grip on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate after it relaxed Friday a number of restrictions governing foreign banks' access to the fledgling Singapore dollar debt market. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities have bought a lot" of U.S. dollars, said one trader. "They want to make it clear that the new regulations are not meant to internationalize the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The MAS has repeatedly bought U.S. dollars over recent months in order to block any unwelcome appreciation of the Singapore dollar, which may threaten the competitiveness of the island republic's exports. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading Monday, the U.S. dollar was being quoted at S$1.6765, up from S$1.6710 Friday. So heavy was the buying Monday that one dealer at a Japanese bank in Singapore suggested the U.S. dollar was likely to be pushed to a six-week high above S$1.6800 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won strengthened as Korean asset markets continued to attract heavy fund inflows of around $50 million a day from foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  Won bulls were further encouraged by comments made over the weekend by the country's trade minister, Han Duck-soo, who said the won's strength hasn't damaged South Korean export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Some observers interpreted the remark as a sign that Seoul's hard line on won appreciation may be softening. \par \par \uc2  At Monday's close the dollar was quoted against the won at KRW1,159.00 won, down from Friday's close of KRW1,160.20. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also strengthened Monday in heavy trading. Intraday dealing saw the U.S. dollar sold as low as NT$31.693, its lowest level in two years, before it recovered slightly to end the session at NT$31.700, down a fraction from NT$31.724 Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbt08474\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
