\par\par \b \uc2  DATA VIEW: Singapore GDP Growth May Bolster  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 1999\par 02:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Singapore's economic growth maintained its brisk annual pace of 6.7% in the third quarter, prompting some economists to predict the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen. \par \par \uc2  GDP growth was unchanged from the second quarter and slightly below the 6.9% average forecast of economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires. But it was strong enough to prompt the government to raise its full-year 1999 growth forecast to the top end of its 4% to 5% range. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar gained slightly on the news. At 0152 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6670, down from S$1.6697. Share prices, which generally look to Wall Street for direction, opened lower in response to worries that U.S. inflation could rise on higher oil prices, but have bounced back. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Straits Times Index is up 10.95 points at 2239.81. \par \par \uc2  Simon Flint, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Hong Kong, said strong growth coupled with a slight increase in inflation will prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore to allow the Singapore dollar to strengthen. \par \par \uc2  The consumer price index rose at an annual rate of 0.9% in the third quarter, up from just 0.1% in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  In the past year, the MAS has tried to nurture economic recovery by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar from rising to a level that would hurt exports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts monetary policy in Singapore by guiding the exchange rate rather than interest rates. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would make imported products cheaper, putting downward pressure on inflation. \par \par \uc2  "As growth is restored you'll see a tightening of monetary policy and hence an appreciation of the Singapore dollar," Flint said. \par \par \uc2  Rising oil prices will push up the inflation rate next year, Ho Meng Kit, the deputy secretary of the Ministry of Trade and Industry, said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Singapore likely will see inflation between 1% and 2% next year, up from a forecast inflation rate of 0.5% in 1999, Ho said. \par \par \uc2  Manufacturing output continued to lead economic growth, rising by 16.7% in the third quarter, compared with a gain of 14.5% in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Electronics sector output jumped by 26%, driven largely by exports of telecommunications equipment and semiconductors, the ministry said. But the disk drive segment continued to languish as major producers moved \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower-cost countries. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say lagging disk-drive exports are being replaced by rising shipments of specialty chemicals and pharmaceuticals. \par \par \uc2  The construction industry remained weak, with output falling by 15.6%, an even steeper decline than the 13.3% drop in the second quarter. Contracts awarded in the third quarter, an indicator of future activity, fell by 44%. \par \par \uc2  Economists and policymakers said the overall outlook for Singapore's economy remains good as growth stays steady or accelerates in its major export markets - the U.S., Japan and the European Union. \par \par \uc2  Friedrich Wu, chief economist at the Development Bank of Singapore, said manufacturing investments, an indicator of future activity, rose by 22% in the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  He predicted growth this year would exceed the government's 5% forecast and would come close to the top of the 4.5% to 6.5% range that the government expects in 2000. \par \par \uc2  -By Christopher Wellisz; 65 421-4802; christopher.wellisz@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbi07zz5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
