\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 : Won Soars To 4-Mo High; Rupiah Tumbles \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 1999\par 10:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Soaring market sentiment drove the South Korean won to its highest level in over four months Thursday, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suppress the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. \par \par \uc2  Asia's other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fared less well, as market concerns over Aceh's calls for independence plunged the rupiah to its lowest level since Indonesia's presidential elections three weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  The nervousness spilt over to affect other South East Asian markets, dragging the Singapore dollar, Thai baht and Philippine peso lower. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward South Korea, already riding high after a long-expected liquidity crisis failed to materialize, was further boosted Thursday when ratings agency Standard & Poor's Corp. upgraded the country's sovereign ratings, citing the strength of its economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The upgrade triggered heavy buying of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which briefly pushed the dollar to a four-month low of 1,169.70 won before the Bank of Korea stepped into the market to buy dollars in a rare direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Continued strong portfolio capital inflows from abroad and a good supply of dollars to the market from local exporters have led to intense appreciative pressure on the won in the last few days," explained David Simmonds, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Not surprisingly, we have seen official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels," added Simmonds. "Government sensitivity to the role of the export sector as the key driver of economic growth is still strong." \par \par \uc2  By the end of trading in Seoul, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lifted the dollar from its earlier lows. But closing at KRW1,173.10, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained well below Wednesday's close of KRW1,180.00. \par \par \uc2  Although the South Korean authorities won't attempt to reverse the won's appreciation over the long term, further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the pace of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise is probable in the near future, warned Simmonds at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  With the market already heavily long in the won, and investment inflows unlikely to continue at their current pace, the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance is set to be erratic, he said. \par \par \uc2  Investment inflows and exporters' sales of U.S. dollars also lifted the New Taiwan dollar Thursday. At the close of dealing in Taipei, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at NT$31.745, down from NT$31.754 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the market's growing concern that calls for independence by the North Sumatran province of Aceh could evolve into a fresh Indonesian crisis triggered heavy buying of dollars against the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Market participants who went long in the rupiah at the time of Indonesia's presidential elections three weeks ago anticipating a major post-election rally were seen buying dollars to cover their positions. \par \par \uc2  In conditions of thin market liquidity, the buying rapidly pushed the dollar above the 7,000 rupiah mark to reach IDR7,150 late in Asian trading, up over IDR250 compared with IDR6,897 late the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's fall soured sentiment toward other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, dragging the Thai baht, the Singapore dollar and the Philippine peso all lower. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also cited Thailand's political backdrop as a reason to sell the baht, saying an opposition no-confidence motion tabled for next month was deterring foreign investment in the country. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading, the dollar had risen to 39.0450 baht, up from THB38.9600 the day before. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar also slipped in line with the regional trend, with some traders suggesting that market participants caught long in the rupiah were attempting to hedge their exposure by selling the Singapore dollar short. \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Asian interbank dealing, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted against the Singapore dollar at S$1.6763, up from S$1.6723 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso also closed down, with the U.S. dollar rising to end trading in Manila at 40.323 pesos, up from PHP40.270 at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbb07xr7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
