\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Open Higher As Rupiah Rally Continues \b0\par\par\uc2 286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 1999\par 03:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- The rupiah has continued to strengthen, pulling other South East Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher in early dealing Friday. \par \par \uc2  Interest to buy the rupiah remains healthy following Thursday's election of popular opposition party leader Megawati Sukarnoputri as Indonesia's vice president, and the election of moderate Islamic cleric Abdurrahman Wahid as president Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  With the rupiah enjoying a honeymoon period among \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and international investors, many market players are predicting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make further gains over the short term, with the U.S. dollar falling as low as 6,500 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  At 0045 GMT (8:45 p.m. Thursday EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted against the Indonesian rupiah at IDR6,850, compared with IDR7,000 late in Asia the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht, the U.S. dollar was quoted at THB39.1250, down a touch from THB39.1350 late the day before. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6689, compared with S$1.6690 toward the end of Asian trading the previous day. Despite fears of dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders in the island republic report no significant dollar-buying interest from local banks, the MAS's usual agents. \par \par \uc2  Against the South Korean won, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at KRW1,204.90, down from KRW1,205.40 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Domestic trading in the Philippine peso and the New Taiwan dollar has yet to begin. At Thursday's close, the U.S. dollar was at PHP40.175 and NT$31.766. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvam07klp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
