\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Give Up Early Gains To End Mixed Vs Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 1999\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Early gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were whittled away late in regional trading hours to leave regional markets mixed on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, the Singapore dollar, and the Indonesian rupiah all advanced strongly in early trading, only to succumb to profit-taking later in the day. The Philippine peso also slipped in late trading to end the session lower. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia the Korean won strengthened modestly, while the New Taiwan dollar ended unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fall against the yen and the euro, impelled by Friday's fall in U.S. equity prices, was the chief reason cited for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early decline against Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's bias is to the downside across the board," said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banque Paribas in Singapore, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  softened during morning trading. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, the market hesitated to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down too aggressively, leading some traders to conclude that any further dollar losses would be limited. \par \par \uc2  "Regional markets have been led by the baht, but the (dollar against the) baht is looking oversold now," said one trader at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  In early Asian trading the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sold down to test an intraday low against the baht of THB39.0800 before rebounding as traders bought dollars back again to take profits from their short positions. \par \par \uc2  Towards the end of Asian interbank dealing, the buying had pushed the dollar as high as THB39.3300, higher than THB39.2600 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar also fell against the Singapore dollar during the Asian morning, although the fear of provoking dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  deterred traders from pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below an intraday low around S$1.6720. \par \par \uc2  With the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen as well supported above S$1.6700, position liquidation lifted the U.S. dollar in afternoon trading, to leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair quoted at S$1.6754 late in Asian hours, down from S$1.6777 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Trading in the peso followed a similar pattern, with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opening down, only to recover to end the session higher, at PHP40.440, up from PHP40.330 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah, however, managed to hold onto most of the ground it gained, although it failed to extend the advances made in early trading. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta and Singapore reported interest to buy the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming from offshore players seeking to establish long-rupiah positions in expectation of an extended rally should opposition leader Megawati Sukarnoputri capture the presidency in Wednesday's elections. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's gains were limited, however, by firm dollar-buying interest which supported the dollar at the IDR7,800 level. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar had recovered from its earlier low to trade at IDR7,865, down from IDR8,050 Friday. \par \par \uc2  In North Asian markets, the South Korean won closed higher after gaining in line with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  during early trading. \par \par \uc2  At the end of domestic trading in Seoul, the U.S. dollar was at KRW1,203.40, lower than Friday's closing level of at KRW1,206.00. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed unchanged from Friday at NT$31.765. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvai07kmc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
