\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Strengthen; Rupiah Flat Despite Unrest \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 1999\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened robustly against the U.S. dollar during local trading hours on Friday, despite lingering fears that worsening unrest in Indonesia could generate increasing regional contagion. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah itself closed local trading flat compared to levels seen late Thursday, having rallied strongly from its earlier intraday low. \par \par \uc2  Market attention throughout Asia remains closely focused on the political process in Jakarta ahead of Indonesia's presidential election next Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Early dealings on Friday saw the rupiah sold down steeply on fears of violent protests following President Habibie's so-called "accountability speech" late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But, as on Thursday, the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall was arrested by rupiah purchases executed by a handful of European banks acting in both Singapore and Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Traders in both centers said the banks were positioning themselves long in the rupiah in anticipation that an election victory for opposition leader Megawati Sukarnoputri would trigger a sharp rally in the rupiah toward the end of next week. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah-buying drove the dollar off its early high of IDR8,200, back down to IDR8,050 before trading in Jakarta ground to a halt as many dealers left the city center early, anxious to avoid running foul of street demonstrations protesting against Habibie's candidacy. Late Thursday in Asia, the dollar had also been quoted at IDR8,050. \par \par \uc2  In other regional markets, the Thai baht gained more ground on the U.S. dollar as a liquidity squeeze in the offshore market forced a number of players to buy baht in order to cover short forward positions. \par \par \uc2  Offshore baht liquidity has been hard to come by since last week when the Bank of Thailand tightened restrictions governing nonresidents' access to baht funding. \par \par \uc2  Friday saw offshore tomorrow/next forward rates rise to a premium of 0.15 sen over the spot rate according to one Singapore dealer, in stark contrast to the discount of 0.02 sen quoted in the onshore market. \par \par \uc2  The baht's rise was reinforced by an inflow of foreign funds associated with Friday's recapitalization of Thai Farmers Bank, which is expected to have raised up to $300 million from offshore investors. \par \par \uc2  By late in Asian trading, the U.S. dollar had slipped to THB39.26, down from THB39.42 the day before. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also eased against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, although fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to block excessive local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation supported the U.S. dollar above S$1.6750. \par \par \uc2  Late in local trading hours, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6777, down from S$1.6805 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso closed flat, with the U.S. dollar ending domestic trading in Manila unchanged from Thursday at PHP40.330. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won closed higher, aided by Friday's rise in the yen and boosted by a late rush of pre-weekend position squaring, despite dollar purchases executed by state-run banks. \par \par \uc2  At the Seoul close, the U.S. dollar was trading at KRW1,206.00, down from KRW1,208.50 at the end of Thursday's session. \par \par \uc2  Active \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  participation in the Taipei \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market helped to hold the new Taiwan dollar within a narrow trading range. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading Friday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at NT$31.769, down fractionally from NT$31.771 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvaf07hol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
