\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -2: S Asia Tensions Source Of Concern \b0\par\par\uc2  By late in Asian trading, the dollar had been bid as high as IDR8,080, up over 3% from IDR7,827 late the previous day. \par \uc2 237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 1999\par 10:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the most part took a back seat to the rupiah, but the Singapore dollar, the Thai baht and the Philippine peso all ended the day lower against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although the coup in Pakistan wasn't a major factor influencing trading, some dealers and analysts did mention fears of rising tensions between Pakistan and India as a reason to buy dollars against Asian currnecies. \par \par \uc2  Any escalation in border clashes between the two nuclear powers is likely to take some of the shine off Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general, as nervous investors trim their exposure to the region as a whole. \par \par \uc2  The fall in the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was attributed to U.S. banks, which were said by traders to be buying U.S. dollars following \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The MAS is widely believed to have entered the market to buy U.S. dollars Tuesday, placing a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below S$1.6750. \par \par \uc2  Late on Wednesday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6839, up from S$1.6791 toward the end of Asian trading the day before. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 13-10-99 \par \par \uc2  0856GMT \par \par