\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Baht, Peso, NT Dlr Higher \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Truscott \par \uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 1999\par 10:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed against the U.S. dollar in late trading Tuesday, as markets regained some normalcy following a Monday holiday in the U.S., Canada and Japan that muted market activity. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the close correlation between regional and major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also weakened in past sessions, meaning the Thai baht has been providing direction for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  That proved to be the case for the Philippine peso, which moved higher in early trading, traders said, mirroring the Thai baht's sharp move up against the U.S. dollar during the session. The Indonesian rupiah languished in thin trading, because of the political uncertainty in the run-up to presidential elections. \par \par \uc2  Only the Singapore dollar managed to take centerstage Tuesday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was thought to have indirectly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through local banks, pushing the U.S. dollar as high as S$1.6802 from an intraday low of S$1.6732. \par \par \uc2  "The way the local banks started bidding looked like a typical (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) move by the MAS," a trader at a U.S. bank in Singapore said, adding that the move was also accompanied by aggressive bidding by foreign banks, including Natwest Bank. \par \par \uc2  At 0800 GMT (4:00 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6790, up from S$1.6773 in late Asian trading Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a key level in coming sessions will be S$1.6830, a level the U.S. dollar failed to break through earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, it's a tug of war. We're seeing some selling pressure above S$1.68 by corporates, and U.S. dollar buying every time it dips towards S$1.6730 or so," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at a local bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 12-10-99 \par \par \uc2  0833GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvac07cpe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
