\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -2: Peso Dragged Lower By Baht's Losses \b0\par\par\uc2 270 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1999\par 10:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise ran out of steam in afternoon trading, however, after Thai exporters, who had earlier lifted offers placed at THB38.40, resubmitted them at THB38.75. \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Asian interbank trading, the U.S. dollar had settled at THB38.7600, up from THB38.3700 late the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The fall of the baht had little impact on the region's other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The start of afternoon trading in Manila saw the peso briefly dragged lower by the baht's slide, but the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered some of its losses by the end of the session. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the U.S. dollar was quoted at 39.700 pesos, up from PHP39.670 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah also ended lower, but market participants said the market was drifting sideways in a broad range, with little trading interest seen. \par \par \uc2  After falling to test support at 7,600 rupiah, the dollar rose to meet resistance around IDR7,725. Toward the end of Asian dealing, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being quoted at IDR7,707, up from IDR7,660 late in Asia the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar spent most of the session ranging sideways, despite the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall against the yen. \par \par \uc2  With local banks said to be bidding for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at S$1.6800, traders shied away from testing the strength of the U.S. dollar's support, lest they