\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Unwinding US Long-Dollar Positions \b0\par\par\uc2 220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 1999\par 11:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar is slightly stronger against the U.S. dollar late Monday as players square last week's long U.S. dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  About 0920 GMT (5:20 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6866, down from S$1.6888 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a local bank said fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had prompted players to take long U.S. dollar positions last week. As fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have dissipated, these long U.S. dollar positions were now unwinding as the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued its rise against the U.S. dollar in tandem with the strengthening yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar-Sing is overbought," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the U.S. dollar is likely to remain in a tight range of S$1.6850 to S$1.6900 for the rest of the week in the absence of fresh market factors. \par \par \uc2  In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  money market, the overnight rate was offered at 1.375%, down from 1.500% late Friday, while the three-month rate was offered at 2.375%, down from 2.675%. \par \par \uc2  -By Anita Jain; 65-421-4807; anita.jain@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 30-08-99 \par \par \uc2  0949GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8u06djo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
