\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Weaken Late Despite Rise In Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 1999\par 10:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened against the U.S. dollar during local trading hours Monday, even though the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself fell against the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was the only regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to benefit from the yen's strength. Others, including the Philippine peso, the Thai baht, the New Taiwan dollar and the Korean won, all ended the day lower. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore took heart from the rise of the yen to bid up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , assuming that, with the yen strong, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be less inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar down against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Last week, according to traders, the MAS bought U.S. dollars at S$1.6780 to maintain the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's neutrality against the trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  it tracks. \par \par \uc2  Monday, however, as the U.S. dollar dropped to a fresh five-month low within a whisker of Y114.00, there was no sign of the MAS in the market. \par \par \uc2  By late in Asian hours the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had slipped to S$1.6799, down from S$1.6864 toward the end of local trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  Other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fared less well, with both the Thai baht and the Philippine peso falling steeply against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The drop in the baht had many traders scratching their heads. Some suggested that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline was a result of the rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sizable dollar purchases against the baht, they said, were being driven by large U.S. and European players unwinding long positions in the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had been funded with cheap short positions in the yen and the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 02-08-99 \par \par \uc2  0838GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8205qy1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
