\par\par \b \uc2  US Dlr/MAS -2: US Dlr At S$1.6900 \b0\par\par\uc2 168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 1999\par 04:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore is believed to have entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to shore up the sagging U.S. dollar, pushing it above the key S$1.6800 level Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  At 0230 GMT (10:30 p.m. Wednesday EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6900, sharply higher than S$1.6775 about half an hour earlier and S$1.6820 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say local banks are buying U.S. dollars, which is believed to be at the behest of the MAS. \par \par \uc2  Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not be reached immediately. MAS rarely confirms individual \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying only that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  from time to time to smooth market volatility. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has been trading the past few days at six-month lows against the Singapore dollar in line with its weakness against the yen. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 29-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0253GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7t05lz8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
