\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-S'pore sees 1999 GDP forecast revised up. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dan Sloan \par \uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 1999\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 28 (Reuters) - Singapore's economy enjoyed better-than-expected growth in the second quarter of 1999 and an upward revision in full-year growth is coming, the nation's deputy prime minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The second quarter has turned out better than we had expected," Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong told Reuters Television in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "We had a very good quarter and I think we have to recognise this and make a realistic forecast for what we expect for the year," he said. \par \par \uc2  GDP for 1999 is currently forecast between zero and two percent higher than last year. The Singapore government is expected to announce a new forecast on National Day, August 9. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's total manufacturing output surged 18.4 percent during the 12 months to June, the government said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Exports for June showed 7.5 percent annual growth. \par \par \uc2  In July, Singapore announced changes in the way it calculated gross domestic product and revised down growth estimates for 1998 and the first quarter of 1999. \par \par \uc2  GDP for 1998 is now seen 0.3 percent higher than in the previous full year, rather than 1.5 percent, while the first quarter is estimated 0.8 percent higher than a year earlier, rather than 1.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the deputy prime minister said he did not think the changes would be significant to the overall 1999 figure. \par \par \uc2  "It affects only the financial sector. It may make a half a percentage point (difference) or so," Lee said. \par \par \uc2  He said that when an earlier 1999 growth forecast of minus one to plus one percent was made in November last year, it reflected an overall sense of uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  "Minus one to plus one really meant 'we can't say, we don't know, this is a stab in the dark,'" Lee said. \par \par \uc2  "Then we had a fairly good first quarter so we revised it up (to the current range). Now we've had an even better second quarter, so we revise it up again." \par \par \uc2  Singapore has been criticised as being too conservative in its growth forecasts, but Lee said this was to avoid having to make revisions downward later. \par \par \uc2  "We are conservative on what we publicly commit to being certain of delivering. I think that is a sensible, sober approach," he said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's equity market had steamed ahead until early July as investors sensed an improving economic outlook, but Lee said the rally had exceeded the improvement in the underlying economy. \par \par \uc2  "The change in the stock market far exceeded the change in the fundamentals. Maybe it was a bit undervalued before, perhaps it's a bit overvalued now, probably a mix of both," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore stock market has tumbled more than seven percent since reaching a record high of 2,247.43 on July 5. The key Straits Times Index ended at 2,077.65 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Lee also said he was "very comfortable" with the Singapore dollar level on a trade-weighted basis. \par \par \uc2  "On a trade weighted basis, we are very comfortable, so I don't think that anything important happened yesterday." \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar rose to a six-month high on Tuesday of around 1.6755 per dollar before edging back. \par \par \uc2  Some traders suspected that the MAS had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains. \par \par \uc2  ((\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  Newsroom (65) 870-3080; Fax (65) 776-8112, singapore.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \