\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Rally; Singapore Dlr Touches 6-Mo High \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1999\par 10:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The continued strength of the yen helped other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rally against the U.S. dollar during local trading hours Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Singapore dollar and the baht both rose strongly against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In North Asia, the Korean won firmed as local markets recovered from the sell-off seen in recent days. \par \par \uc2  The yen's overnight rally, coupled with unexpectedly strong industrial production numbers for June, pushed the Singapore dollar higher to touch a six-month high briefly during Tuesday's trade. \par \par \uc2  Spooked by the yen's overnight rise, traders moved to unwind short positions in the Singapore dollar against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Interbank dealers soon jumped on the trend, selling the U.S. dollar short in an attempt to trigger stop-loss orders below S$1.6800. The sales did briefly push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a six-month low at S$1.6780, but at that level local banks emerged, aggressively bidding the U.S. dollar back up above S$1.6800. \par \par \uc2  "They looked quite determined," remarked an interbank trader, echoing the market's belief that the bids had been executed on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The MAS seldom \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  openly, although last month the authority did admit buying U.S. dollars to arrest an overly rapid appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As Asian interbank trading drew to a close, the U.S. dollar had been pushed up from its earlier low to trade at S$1.6850. Late Monday the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at S$1.6973. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 27-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0835GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7r05kfc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
