\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 : New Taiwan Dollar's Risk Premium Mounts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 1999\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The risk premium on the New Taiwan dollar mounted steeply Wednesday, as political tensions across the Taiwan strait deepened. \par \par \uc2  Although \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited the extent of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell-off in the Taipei market, dealers in the offshore market responded to the crisis by aggressively bidding up the premium on non-deliverable U.S. dollar/New Taiwan dollar forwards. \par \par \uc2  With market attention focused squarely on the escalating tensions between Taiwan and China, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere in the region enjoyed a day of relatively quiet trading. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was pushed lower by government-directed dollar purchases, but the Thai baht, the Philippine peso and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar all edged higher. The Indonesian rupiah softened. \par \par \uc2  Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was estimated to have sold well over $250 million in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , managed to nudge the New Taiwan dollar only a fraction higher as deteriorating sentiment and a steep 4% slide in the local stock market weighed heavily on the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  The sell-off was prompted by an escalating war of words between Taipei and Beijing. Relations between the two governments took an abrupt turn for the worse after Taiwanese President Lee Teng-hui told a German radio station that island aims to conduct dealings with the mainland on a state-to-state basis, explicitly claiming for Taiwan a statehood status that China refuses to recognize. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar sell-off drove the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to an intraday high of NT$32.330 Wednesday before the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market and began selling U.S. dollars. By the end of the trading session the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been driven back down to a level of NT$32.272, a touch below Tuesday's closing level of NT$32.275. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 14-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0830GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7e058wu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
