\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS-China-Taiwan brouhaha rattles stocks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 924 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 1999\par 11:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 14 (Reuters) - Regional stocks took a bit of \par \par \uc2  a pasting on Wednesday with Taipei and North Asia markets badly \par \par \uc2  mauled by concern over the mounting tension between Taiwan and \par \par \uc2  China. \par \par \uc2  Taipei stocks reeled to close nearly four percent lower as \par \par \uc2  the political punch-up with China over the scrapping of Taiwan's \par \par \uc2  one-China policy sent stocks lower for a second day. \par \par \uc2  "It's obviously a huge concern and as we look forward, \par \par \uc2  sentiment is likely to remain poor," said Steve Brice, regional \par \par \uc2  treasury economist at Standard Chartered in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Whether we see any official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for stocks seems \par \par \uc2  reasonably unlikely, they were looking for a moderation of the \par \par \uc2  stock market rise anyway." \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI TOTTERS \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's weighted index dropped through the psychologically \par \par \uc2  important 8,000 level and broke below some important chart \par \par \uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the stand-off with China was undoubtedly the \par \par \uc2  main driving force behind the slide but investors were using it \par \par \uc2  as an excuse to take profits, particularly in technology issues. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan authorities are quite capable of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly \par \par \uc2  in the markets and the finance ministry did not rule this out. \par \par \uc2  "The government has a tool in hand," finance minister Paul \par \par \uc2  Chiu said. "The tool is stabilisation fund. We do not rule out \par \par \uc2  the possibility of using it when necessary." \par \par \uc2  In 1996 when China staged "war games" in the Taiwan Straits \par \par \uc2  around election time in Taiwan, the markets sagged alarmingly \par \par \uc2  but the authorities moved in aggressively to prop them up. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN WOES RATTLE NORTH ASIA STOCKS \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng was not immune to the China/Taiwan situation, \par \par \uc2  giving up nearly three percent, with China plays sharply lower \par \par \uc2  on fresh concern about a devaluation of the yuan after an \par \par \uc2  apparent softening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's line on the subject. \par \par \uc2  But analysts do not see a move by China anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  "There won't be any immediate Chinese devaluation," said \par \par \uc2  Bill Overhalt, Asia strategist at Nomura International in Hong \par \par \uc2  Kong. \par \par \uc2  "The Prime Minister is concerned about a combination of low \par \par \uc2  interest rates, a lot of fiscal stimulus and a potential \par \par \uc2  devaluation sparking off another round of inflation." \par \par \uc2  "They are going to hold tight and see how the other policies \par \par \uc2  play out first." \par \par \uc2  More foreign cash flowed out of the Seoul stock market which \par \par \uc2  extended its two day losing streak. \par \par \uc2  THAI STOCKS FRET OVER BANK EARNINGS \par \par \uc2  Thai markets were jittery over forthcoming bank earnings \par \par \uc2  with financial issues leading the stock market lower. \par \par \uc2  Thai commercial banks are seen reporting further heavy \par \par \uc2  quarterly losses next week but some of the larger institutions \par \par \uc2  could see a return to profitability next year. \par \par \uc2  Sector analysts expect the banks to post a combined loss of \par \par \uc2  60 billion baht ($1.62 billion) for the April-June period, their \par \par \uc2  seventh hefty quarterly setback since the baht's devaluation. \par \par \uc2  This compares with an actual 46 billion baht net loss the \par \par \uc2  previous quarter and a 104.4 billion baht loss for the period \par \par \uc2  from October to December last year. \par \par \uc2  But consumer confidence seems to be picking up in Thailand, \par \par \uc2  with a survey showing that 24.3 percent of Thais expected their \par \par \uc2  incomes to improve in the next six months. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  HAVE FIRMER BIAS \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a firmer bias but the Korean won bucked the \par \par \uc2  trend as banks continued to suck up dollars for loan loss \par \par \uc2  provisioning. \par \par \uc2  This has been overshadowing the market ever since the \par \par \uc2  government advanced the deadline for banks to cover overseas \par \par \uc2  loan losses to the end of September in a move expected to drain \par \par \uc2  around $2 billion dollars from the market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Change on the day WED 14 0901GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest bid....Prev Close....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit......3.7995.......3.8000.........0.01% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah......6675.00......6640.00........-0.52% \par \par \uc2   Baht..........37.07........37.19.........0.32% \par \par \uc2   Peso..........38.20........38.30.........0.26% \par \par \uc2   Sing dlr.....1.6985.......1.7010.........0.15% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan dlr...32.280.......32.235........-0.14% \par \par \uc2   Korean won..1183.00......1175.50........-0.63% \par \par \uc2   Japan yen....121.13.......122.14.........0.83% \par \par \uc2   Change since start of Asia crisis \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .....Latest......July '97......Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit......3.7995.......2.5200........-33.68%