\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Fund View: CMG First State Sees Stock Rally Extending \b0\par\par\uc2  By Camille Klass \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 1999\par 12:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asia's stock markets have been on the rise since late last year, prompting some to wonder whether a reversal may be in the offing. But, CMG First State Investments Singapore expects the region's bourses to continue their upward trend. \par \par \uc2  "The opportunities for Asia, we feel, are long haul," said Kim Teo, chief executive officer for the fund manager, Thursday. "Asian markets have been very productive, but we feel that it's not a short-term blip." \par \par \uc2  For CMG First State Investments Singapore, Asia remains the most attractive investment region despite the fact that stock markets from Korea to the Philippines have gained between 30% and 75% since January. \par \par \uc2  That's the main reason the company relaunched its two Asian equity funds last week even though the sharp region-wide rally is almost eight months old. \par \par \uc2  The two funds - one investing broadly in the region, the other investing in Singapore - were previously managed by Nicholas-Applegate Capital Management, which was acquired in May by Australia's Colonial group. CMG First State Investments, of which Nicholas-Applegate is now a part, is Colonial's fund management arm. \par \par \uc2  While there is a chance that the bourses could retreat in coming months, post-crisis changes in many regional markets are expected to translate into real economic benefits that will help prolong the rally, said associate director Yeo Boon Hong. \par \par \uc2  "I think generally Asian markets will continue to benefit from the reforms and restructuring in 1997 and 1998," Yeo said. "If you think of those years as building years, we should be able to see sustained earnings recovery in 2000." \par \par \uc2  Increasing paid-up capital of companies, lower and falling interest rates, reduced corporate debt and a gradual revival of earnings are among these changes, he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moreover, Asian companies have started to pay greater attention to\par return on equity and enhancing shareholder value, themes that are\par helping to underpin stock market gains, Yeo said.\par              Liquidity Not Expected To Dry Up\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Singapore, for instance, the return on equity for companies in\par general is between 5% and 10%. While the level is similar to that in\par 1996, when the benchmark Straits Times Index was also hovering just\par above the 2000 mark, Yeo said the situation is now different. While the\par return on equity was on its way down in 1996, it is now headed upward,\par he said, justifying the Singapore bourse's strong advance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some have expressed concerns that liquidity will begin to dry up now\par that stock prices in some Asian countries are well above their\par pre-crisis levels, but Yeo disagreed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The flow of money - overseas and local - which has been the primary\par driver of regional stock gains, is in no danger of ebbing, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Citing recent market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of Korea to depress their local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Yeo\par said the moves suggest fund flows into Asia remain strong.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I believe liquidity will be here, and going forward that will be\par supported by earnings growth," he said. "So, we're fairly optimistic on\par Asian markets."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gains are likely to remain broad-based, Yeo said, but as individual\par markets are at different stages of reform, he said he especially favors\par the stock markets of South Korea, Singapore and Thailand.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The restructuring process has been most prominent in South Korea,\par where the paid-up capital of many companies has doubled this year, and\par where the stock market is at a 40-month high, Yeo said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par South Korea, blazing the trail for markets in the region, is up around\par 78% so far this year. Singapore's Straits Times Index is up about 55%,\par and Thailand has gained around 40%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the gains seem unusually steep, and may suggest further\par increases will be hard to come by, Yeo suggested there's still room for\par the markets to climb given the return of investor confidence.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "You must keep the market's rise in perspective," he said. "The\par markets were very oversold; they were disliked and disowned. But, there\par has been a drastic change in sentiment."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Camille Klass; 65-421-4801; cklass@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1139 55129 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv78031wf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
