\par\par \b \uc2  MAS move to delay, not defeat Singapore dollar rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1999\par 10:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 7 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar ignored a rare admission by local authorities on Wednesday that they had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market is convinced the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon head higher. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (MAS) confirmation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would discourage players from pushing the local dollar up too abruptly, but it showed limited downside given gains in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "With growth and inflation returning to positive territory and the rapid stock market turnaround threatening to fuel more consumer price gains, the argument for a depreciating Sing dollar is rapidly deteriorating," said Thio Chin Loo, strategist at Paribas. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar stood at 1.6968/78 to the U.S. dollar in late Asian trade, little changed from its early levels after the MAS knocked it from an overnight high of 1.6870. \par \par \uc2  The MAS said it had stepped in on Tuesday to curb the Singapore dollar's gains because the speed of its rise was unwarranted and seemed at risk of getting ahead of the economy. \par \par \uc2  It rose sharply on Tuesday after the government said the economy was recovering faster than expected. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strengthened less than three percent from its 1999 trough of 1.7390 to the U.S. dollar, compared with gains of 3.5 percent to 40 percent in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Even the stolid Taiwan dollar, which is frequently reined in by its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has gained 3.5 percent from its year low of 33.72 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Singapore and Taiwan compete closely in export markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Singapore dollar was likely to trade in a 1.69-1.70 range for the time-being, with a raft of offshore dollar offers above 1.70 limiting its declines. \par \par \uc2  "Offshore players who are unwinding some of their carry trades have good interest to sell (dollars) above 1.7000," said a senior regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has been under pressure as traders took advantage of low domestic interest rates to use it as a funding tool for carry trades, positioning themselves long Philippine peso/short Sing for instance. \par \par \uc2  But Thio at Paribas said such trades now looked less attractive as interest rates were on the decline throughout Asia. \par \par \uc2  "These are slowly being reversed. And because the Sing dollar has been a laggard in terms of appreciation, it could now perform better than the others," she said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared aimed at slowing the pace of the Sing dollar's rise, rather than trying to reverse it. \par \par \uc2  Thio said a target of 1.6850 remained intact for the near term, and the Sing dollar should strengthen to 1.65 further down the road. \par \par \uc2  The MAS also said it would now tighten its exchange rate bands to levels it maintained before the Asian crisis sparked huge volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Singapore maintains a tight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, managing its exchange rate against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and within a band. It does not publish information about the basket or the band. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the decision to narrow the band suggested greater confidence in the stability of financial markets, but added that it could also be a warning that more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely. \par \par \uc2  "While there is the expectation that the recovery process is in place and that is helping to dampen volatility, there are still some major risks out there," said Andrew Dermot-Fung, treasury economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  "They have brought (the band) back to pre-crisis levels, which would suggest that they have a slightly increased tendency to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market if volatility does not die down," Fung told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7704xub\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
