\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-S'pore economic recovery prospects good - MAS. \b0\par\par\uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1999\par 07:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 7 (Reuters) - Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday prospects for the domestic economy in the second half of the year onwards were good, but it was too early to conclude it was "totally out of the woods." \par \par \uc2  "Major uncertainties lie ahead," \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) managing director Koh Yong Guan said in the bank's annual report, released on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There are risk factors to watch out for, particularly a sharp correction in the U.S. stock market, a weakening of the Japanese yen and rising protectionist pressures," he said. \par \par \uc2  The robustness of the U.S. economy, signs of recovery in Japan and an increase in production and exports from many crisis-hit economies were encouraging signs, the bank said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong, who is also MAS chairman, said on Tuesday the government would revise its 1999 gross domestic product growth forecast in August as the economy was growing faster than expected. \par \par \uc2  The official forecast is for growth of zero to two percent. The economy grew at 1.2 percent during the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  "Prospects for a sustained economic recovery into the second half of 1999 and beyond are good," Koh said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "The external environment is expected to be favourable and the policy measures taken in 1998 in response to the economic slowdown are beginning to take effect," he said. \par \par \uc2  In its outlook for the economy, the MAS said there were signs that some sectors were bottoming out but cautioned that recovery was not across the board and that downside risk was apparent in industrialised nations and those emerging from the crisis. \par \par \uc2  The MAS said it remained vigilant on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and confirmed that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb a rise in its value against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "It seemed the (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's) move was not warranted and it seemed the speed of the movement was going ahead of the economy," Yeo Laim Sim, MAS assistant managing director of capital markets, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  She said further action would depend on moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and their impact on the trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against which MAS manages the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was traded at 1.6900 to the U.S. dollar late on Tuesday, compared with 1.6970 a day earlier. At 0550 GMT on Wednesday, it was traded at 1.6970/80. \par \par \uc2  The bank said it would tighten its exchange rate bands to levels it had maintained before Asia's economic crisis struck. Koh did not disclose details of the bands. \par \par \uc2  Last year, MAS eased its exchange rate policy to cushion the economy from the crisis and facilitate recovery. Since early this year, the trade-weighted value of the Singapore dollar has been back at pre-crisis levels. \par \par \uc2  The MAS said it would ensure exchange rate policy remained conducive to a sustained non-inflationary recovery. It maintained its inflation forecast for 1999 at zero percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Singapore Newsroom +65 870-3080; Fax +65 776-8112; email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7704xok\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
