\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Lower Early After Reported MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 250 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1999\par 04:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is lower in early trading Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore reportedly sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Tuesday to spook speculators. \par \par \uc2  About 0230 GMT (10:30 p.m. Tuesday EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.6982, well up from S$1.6890 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said MAS came into the market unusually late Tuesday, selling Singapore dollars up to S$1.7005 to the U.S. dollar amid thin volume. \par \par \uc2  "I guess they (MAS) feel the selling (of U.S. dollars against Singapore dollars) was bit overdone, especially with dollar-yen not moving," one trader at a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  The move forced players to cover short U.S.-dollar positions that had been built up as the Singapore dollar strengthened. \par \par \uc2  In early trade Wednesday, the U.S. dollar is expected to hold to a range of roughly S$1.6960 to S$1.6990, he said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see it moving much," the trader said. "A lot of people were hurt yesterday, so it's going to be a very tight range today." \par \par \uc2  On the local money market, the interbank overnight rate was offered at 1.125%, up from 0.875% late Tuesday in Asia, while the three-month rate was offered at 1.6875%, unchanged from late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Roger Malone; 65/421-4800; roger.malone@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0247GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000