\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed; Fed Move Seen Broadly Positive \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 258 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 1999\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a mixed day Thursday. Although the Singapore dollar pushed up through a key resistance level in response to renewed investment flows into the local stock market, both the South Korean won and the Indonesian rupiah ended Asian trading hours weaker. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to raise its federal funds target rate by 25 basis points had little impact on trading, as this rise had long been discounted by regional markets. Less widely expected, however, was the Fed's subsequent reversion to a neutral monetary policy bias - a development interpreted as broadly positive for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Market sentiment favors stronger regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Flows into local stock markets should cap the dollar," a trader at a U.S. bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar benefited the most from this effect Thursday, as sales of the U.S. dollar by local banks pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below the S$1.7000 level at which many dealers had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A trader at a \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank indicated that the MAS had shifted its sights, and that support would now emerge at around S$1.6950, although a further U.S. dollar selloff is probable in European trading hours. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6975, down from S$1.7003 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 01-07-99 \par \par \uc2  0830GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv7104z6d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
