\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Little Changed But Sentiment Strong \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 1999\par 10:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended little changed against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, as expectations of an increase in U.S. interest rates damped activity in local markets. \par \par \uc2  Although the majority of analysts and traders believe a 25-basis point rise in the U.S. federal funds rate target is unlikely to have a significant impact on Asian economies, dealers still trimmed back open positions in the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  just in case the Federal Reserve surprised them. \par \par \uc2  Underlying sentiment toward the regionals remains strong, however, and most traders expect further local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in the second half of the year. \par \par \uc2  The growing belief that regional economic recovery is gathering steam helped push the Singapore dollar slightly higher Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Although the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains were moderate - the market didn't test the S$1.7000 level at which traders believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. dollar - many dealers believe the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to rise further over the longer term. \par \par \uc2  "I favor the (U.S. dollar's) downside from here," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at one of Singapore's big four banks. "The range is intact for the moment, but if the economic data continues to come in positive, it will confirm that Singapore is getting out of the woods and we should see the Singapore dollar strengthen." \par \par \uc2  After recent data releases showed a 16.4% increase in manufacturing output and a 15.5% rise in non-oil domestic exports for May, the chance the Singapore dollar will weaken is "limited" according to the Singapore bank trading chief, who said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would find a solid ceiling at S$1.71. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in Asia, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7003, down from S$1.7044 toward the end of Asian trading hours the previous day. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 30-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0831GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6u04x8l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
